A||||5||bad88||High idle||||5||
Z||000000||bad88||06-04-2000||01:57 PM||||I just replaced my IAC because I decided to remove and clean it for high idle (1800 or so)and I cracked it in the throttle body reinstalling it.....oops.  The idle was much higher with the new one (2500 on start and it will not drop until coolant temp is above 160 degrees) after running it drops to about 1800 again but the driving idle when shifting stays above 2000 rpm for a few moments while shifting.  I checked the vacuum and even connected new hoses to all the lines (not alot of fun with a hot muffler near the throttle body).  When replacing the IAC I cleaned the throttle body and changed the gasket so no leaks there either.   Now the question...When I installed the new IAC the directions said to measure the pintle and screw it in until it was less than 1-1/8" before installing, I screwed it in to 1" so I didn't smash the new one.  I'm wondering if by adjusting that I limited the travel length and now it will not close all the way with the ECM?  Any ideas?  I'm planning on riping it out again to screw that out further again to see if the idle decreases......carbs are so much easier<BR>Brian||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||06-04-2000||02:30 PM||||The IAC needs to be adjusted by the ECM. The way you do it is drive over 40mph for at least 30 seconds. If you had any codes clear them out first. Changing or cleaning an IAC will  produce a high idle until ECM can set it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||06-04-2000||02:42 PM||||I agree with Orief... Did you try driving the car at all since you installed the new IAC?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||bad88||06-04-2000||06:15 PM||||how are you getting away posting while you're working???? Anyway the answer is yes and I did take out the IAC again and it was at about 1-1/2" extended so the ecm is obviously doing something.  I reset it anyway to 1-1/8" and reinstalled, cleared the codes and drove up and down the hwy at 60 for at least a minute each way and still the idle is at 1800 with normal temp.  The book says vacuum but according to the label I have checked all lines and swapped out the hoses just to check for leaks and still no affect with the new hoses or the old ones (idle stays high either way) I tried disconnecting the MAP sensor and the car runs like crap but the idle is right.  I even checked the purge valve on the fuel vapor canister.  I did notice that the IAC looked like new when I pulled it out like it wasn't seating at all.  I figured that there should be some wear when it seats against the bore.  I think I'm going to ask Bill at the garage to look at it tomorrow if he can.  He may have seen this before and he always seems to know more than I have done...maybe that's why HE does that for a living not me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||06-04-2000||07:59 PM||||I always post while working.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Doesn't your manager allow you use the internet?||||reg||1
Z||000005||black-86-GT||06-06-2000||01:39 PM||||Take off and inspect your egr tube. If you are getting a code 35 and a code 32 this is more than likley your problem. I went round and round with this thing I bought a new IAC but that didnt help finally I broke down and took it off and sure enough that was it||||reg||1
